AUDIO: 'perform gestures, and respond to instructions naturally through conversation. This can enable multiple use cases, like an onboarding character that gives virtual tours or interactive'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'characters that respond to users and their surroundings in real time. In this tutorial, we\'ll show you how to set up Convey Actions in Unity, add built-in actions, define objects as targets, and'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bring it all together through natural conversation. Let\'s get started. Before we begin, make sure you already have a working Convey scene and character. If you haven\'t set'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that up yet, please follow our Convey Unity installation and setup tutorial first. Once your character is working correctly, we can start adding actions. First, I\'ll select my Convey character'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the hierarchy. Then, in the Inspector, I\'ll click Add Component, search for Convey Actions, and add the component.'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'ll notice that the component reports a couple of issues because the action runner hasn\'t been set up yet. Let\'s fix that next. There are two ways to set up the action runner.'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can click Add Component again, search for Convey Action Runner, and add it manually. Or, you can click the reported issue and use the Convey Troubleshooter. From here, click Set Up Action Running,'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and Convey will automatically configure the required component for you. Now, our character is ready to use actions. Let\'s add our first action. I\'ll click Open Actions Editor.'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll dock this window here so we can easily access it while configuring and testing our actions. Under Add Your First Action, you\'ll find several'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'built-in actions included with the SDK. For our first example, I\'ll select Follow the Player. As you can see, it automatically added Nav Mesh Agent and Locomotion.'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'When we add the action, its name and description are already configured for us. These fields are important because they help Convey understand when and how the action should be used.'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We can also configure what the character does when the action succeeds or fails. By default, the failure behavior is set to Narrate in Character, while the success behavior is set to Silent.'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can adjust these behaviors depending on the experience you want to create. Follow the player doesn\'t require a separate target. It automatically follows the game object that has the'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Convey Player component. So, make sure the Convey Player component is attached to the player object that moves through your scene. One more thing before we test it. If your action involves locomotion, such as'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'follow the player or walk to, make sure a nav mesh is set up in your scene. Add a nav mesh surface to the ground your character will walk on and bake the'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nav mesh. Your Convey character also needs a nav mesh agent component. For actions that require locomotion, the nav mesh agent is automatically added to the character when needed. With the nav'

